WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.719
The method starts by writing Kernel PCA as the
minimization of a difference of two convex

00:00:05.799 --> 00:00:09.498
functions over the Stiefel manifold of orthonormal frames.

00:00:09.578 --> 00:00:13.928
A general dualization proposition then converts
this primal problem,

00:00:14.008 --> 00:00:18.489
whose variables live in a possibly
infinite-dimensional feature space,

00:00:18.569 --> 00:00:22.594
into a dual problem whose variable H is a finite
n-by-s matrix,

00:00:22.674 --> 00:00:25.004
perfectly suited to gradient descent.

00:00:25.084 --> 00:00:29.304
The indicator function of the Stiefel manifold
turns, in the dual,

00:00:29.384 --> 00:00:32.562
into a nuclear-norm term the authors call pi of H,

00:00:32.642 --> 00:00:36.276
equal to the trace of the square root of H-transpose G H.

00:00:36.356 --> 00:00:41.814
Its gradient only requires the singular value
decomposition of a small s-by-s matrix,

00:00:41.894 --> 00:00:46.244
not the full n-by-n Gram matrix, which is the
source of the speedup.

00:00:46.324 --> 00:00:48.916
For the standard problem they run L-BFGS.

00:00:48.996 --> 00:00:51.131
To promote robustness or sparsity,

00:00:51.211 --> 00:00:57.646
they add a Moreau-envelope term to the objective
and optimize with a difference-of-convex algorithm,

00:00:57.726 --> 00:01:03.380
plugging in closed-form proximal operators for the
Huber and epsilon-insensitive losses.
